STAR-BRICK 01, Version 1.3: 

A Theorem-Driven Theory of Energy-Aware Scheduling for Space-Based Computing — with Time-Indexed Viability Margins, Corrected Thermal Boundaries, and a Resolved Schedulability Obligation

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM


Abstract

This version repairs the margin invariance gap, unifies the energy margin statement, corrects the thermal boundary condition, fixes the ED-H saturation cap, incorporates faults into the disturbance set, corrects the fault-tolerance model, and qualifies the Lyapunov comparator. The central new result is a time-indexed energy viability margin  m_t  defined by backward recursion over the orbit period, with an explicit orbit-balance condition  \sum_{u=0}^{K-1} c_u \le 0  that is necessary and sufficient for a finite periodic margin to exist. The margin is proved controlled-invariant and recursively feasible by induction, resolving the tautology of v1.2 Proposition 5. The thermal margin is redefined with the cooling condition evaluated at the margin boundary rather than at  T^{\max} , and the discretization error is assigned unambiguously to the discrete map rather than double-counted. The ED-H schedulability test is corrected: the saturation cap is removed from the feasibility conditions and replaced by a curtailment-aware usable-energy term, and the processor-demand condition is restored. Faults are placed in the disturbance set  W , so that the discrete-state transition is random and the availability constraint is checked on the successor. The fault-tolerance model is corrected:  q = 0  is the no-recovery case and  q = \infty  is instantaneous recovery; the recursion has base case  P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d \le 0) = 1 ; and the correlated model uses a transient analysis rather than stationary weights. The Lyapunov virtual queues are qualified: the thermal queue is vacuous under the filter, the storage queue enforces only a mean constraint, the stationary comparator requires ergodicity, and the  O(V)  backlog requires a Slater slack. The theorem ledger is updated. No flight readiness, no physical safety beyond the model, and no benefit from LLM assistance without experimental evidence are claimed.

---

1. Introduction

STAR-BRICK 01 v1.2 closed several gaps but introduced or retained others. The most serious was the unproved invariance of the margin: Proposition 3 showed only  \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_B , not  \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B ; Proposition 5 was tautological; and the recursive-feasibility condition of Remark B.4.2 was asserted rather than derived. Secondary issues included three inconsistent statements of the energy margin, a thermal condition evaluated at the wrong point, a saturation cap that constrained feasibility rather than curtailing energy, a conflict between exact discrete observation and random faults, a fault-tolerance model that used stationary weights for finite-duration tasks, and a Lyapunov analysis whose queues were partly vacuous.

This version repairs all of these. The main new result is Section 5: a time-indexed energy viability margin built by backward recursion over the orbit, with a necessary and sufficient orbit-balance condition and a proof of controlled invariance and recursive feasibility. Section 6 corrects the thermal boundary condition. Section 7 redefines the coupled margin. Section 9 corrects the ED-H test. Section 10 qualifies the Lyapunov analysis. Section 11 corrects the fault-tolerance model. Section 12 retains the architecture and reframes the LLM as a heuristic designer. Section 13 updates the theorem ledger.

