2. Mathematical Setting

2.1 State Decomposition

The state space is  X = X^c \times X^d  with

X^c \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_c}, \qquad X^d = \{0,1\}^N \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{G}.

The continuous component  x^c = (B, T, C)  contains stored energy, temperature, and computing capacity. The discrete component  x^d = (A, Q, \mathcal{N})  contains availability, task queue, and communication graph. The metric is

d(x, \hat{x}) = \begin{cases} \|x^c - \hat{x}^c\|_2 & \text{if } x^d = \hat{x}^d, \\ +\infty & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}
\tag{2.1}

The discrete component is observed exactly. The estimator produces  \hat{x}_t \in X  with

x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, \qquad \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon \ge 0.
\tag{2.2}

The estimation error  \varepsilon  is derived from a set-membership observer in Section 5.4 rather than assumed.

2.2 Actions, Admissibility, Robust Admissibility

Let  U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p  be the action space and  \mathcal{A} : X \rightrightarrows U  the admissible action map. The robust admissible set is

\hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in U : a \in \mathcal{A}(x) \ \forall x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\}\bigr\}.
\tag{2.3}

When  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \emptyset , the system enters the safe state (zero-compute, minimal-auxiliary). This is a modeling requirement, not a theorem.

2.3 Disturbances Including Faults

The disturbance is

w_t = (w_t^{\mathrm{gen}}, w_t^{\mathrm{therm}}, w_t^{\mathrm{fault}}) \in W = W^{\mathrm{gen}} \times W^{\mathrm{therm}} \times \{0,1\}^N,
\tag{2.4}

where  w_t^{\mathrm{fault}} \in \{0,1\}^N  is the per-node failure indicator at step  t . Including faults in  W  removes the conflict between exact discrete observation and random failures: the discrete state transition is  A_{t+1} = g_A(A_t, w_t^{\mathrm{fault}}, \text{recovery}) , which is random through  w_t^{\mathrm{fault}} . The reachable set  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a)  is computed over all  w \in W , including all fault patterns, so the filter enforces  \mathcal{S}_A  on every possible successor availability vector. This is the robust approach; the probabilistic fault-tolerance analysis of Section 11 is a separate, complementary guarantee.

2.4 Transition with Saturation Shunt

The continuous component evolves as

B_{t+1} = \min\bigl(B^{\max}, B_t + \Delta t(P_t - L_t)\bigr), \qquad T_{t+1} = T_t + \Delta t\, g(T_t) + e_{\mathrm{disc}},
\tag{2.5}

with  g(T) = (Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T))/C^{\mathrm{th}} . The  \min(B^{\max}, \cdot)  is the shunt: excess generation is curtailed rather than stored. The discretization error  e_{\mathrm{disc}}  belongs to the discrete map  f , not to the continuous plant; it is bounded in Section 6.1 and is not double-counted.

2.5 Safe Set

The safe set  \mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}_B \cap \mathcal{S}_P \cap \mathcal{S}_T \cap \mathcal{S}_C \cap \mathcal{S}_A  is closed, with

\mathcal{S}_B = \{x : 0 \le B_i \le B_i^{\max}\}, \quad \mathcal{S}_T = \{x : T_i \le T_i^{\max}\}, \quad \mathcal{S}_A = \{x : A_i = 0 \Rightarrow \text{no work on } i\}.
\tag{2.6}

2.6 Reachable Set and Filter

\mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) = \bigl\{f(x, a, w) : x^c \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c),\ x^d = \hat{x}^d,\ w \in W\bigr\},
\tag{2.7}

\Phi(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}\bigr\}.
\tag{2.8}

Assumptions 2.1–2.3 (continuity of  f^c , compactness of  W  and  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) , closed graph of  \hat{\mathcal{A}} ) are retained.

