3. Theorem 1: Soundness of the One-Step Safety Filter

Theorem 1. Let Assumptions 2.1–2.3 hold. Suppose  x_0 \in \mathcal{S} , and for every  t \ge 0 :  x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d ,  \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon ,  w_t \in W  almost surely, and  a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t) . Then

\mathbb{P}\bigl[\forall t \ge 0,\ x_t \in \mathcal{S}\bigr] = 1.
\tag{3.1}

Proof. Induction along almost every sample path. Base:  x_0 \in \mathcal{S} . Step: if  x_t \in \mathcal{S}  and  x_t \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}_t^c) \times \{\hat{x}_t^d\} , then  a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t)  gives  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S} , and  w_t \in W  gives  x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S} . The induction closes on  \bigcap_t \{w_t \in W\} , which has probability one.  \square 

The remarks on almost-sure versus deterministic, conditionality, relative soundness, and absence of liveness and optimality carry over from v1.1. Because faults are now in  W , the theorem covers random node failures: the filter checks  \mathcal{S}_A  on every fault-consistent successor.

