4. Recursive Feasibility and the Viability Kernel

4.1 Iterated Viability Operator

\mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}, \qquad \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigl\{x \in \mathcal{S} : \exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x),\ \forall w \in W,\ f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})\bigr\},
\tag{4.1}

with  \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_{k \ge 0} \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) . Under Assumptions 2.1–2.3, each  \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  is closed and the intersection is closed.

Lemma 4.1. If  x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) , there exists a policy  \pi  with  x_t \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S})  for all  t \ge 0 .

Proof. Compactness of  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0)  and closedness of each  \mathrm{Viab}_k  yield a common action  a_0  with  f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S})  for all  w . Induction closes.  \square 

4.2 Margin as Inner Approximation of the Kernel

Proposition 4.2 (Margin-inflation). Let  \mathcal{S}^\circ  be a candidate margin and suppose every  \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ  admits a common action  a  with  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ . Then  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) .

Proof. Controlled invariance of  \mathcal{S}^\circ  makes it a subset of the maximal invariant set.  \square 

The substance of Proposition 4.2 is the controlled-invariance hypothesis, which v1.2 left unproved. Sections 5 and 6 construct margins for which this hypothesis is verified by explicit recursion, not assumed.

