5. Time-Indexed Energy Viability Margin

5.1 Single-Node Dynamics

For a single node,

B_{t+1} = \min\bigl(B^{\max}, B_t + \Delta t(P_t - L_t)\bigr),
\tag{5.1}

with periodic generation  P_t \in [\underline{P}_t, \overline{P}_t]  of period  K  steps (eclipse steps have  \underline{P}_t = 0 ), zero-compute load  L^0_t \in [0, \overline{L}^0_t]  (unavoidable auxiliary load), and estimation error  |B_t - \hat{B}_t| \le \varepsilon . The estimate is projected onto  [0, B^{\max}] , so  \hat{B}_t \in [0, B^{\max}] .

5.2 One-Step Bounds

The one-step safety bound is

a_t = \varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L}^0_t - \underline{P}_t),
\tag{5.2}

the minimum estimate from which the true successor satisfies  B_{t+1} \ge 0 . The worst-case one-step drop of the estimate is

c_t = 2\varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L}^0_t - \underline{P}_t) = a_t + \varepsilon.
\tag{5.3}

The  2\varepsilon  accounts for the fact that the next estimate is within  \varepsilon  of the next true state, which itself is within  \varepsilon  of the current estimate.

5.3 Backward Recursion

Define the time-indexed margin  m_t  by

m_t = \max\bigl(a_t,\ m_{t+1} + c_t\bigr).
\tag{5.4}

The closed form is

m_t = \max_{s \in [t, t+K-1]} \Bigl[a_s + \sum_{u=t}^{s-1} c_u\Bigr].
\tag{5.5}

Proposition 5.1 (Finite periodic solution). A finite periodic solution to (5.4) exists if and only if the orbit balance holds:

\sum_{u=0}^{K-1} c_u \le 0, \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad \sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \underline{P}_u \ge \sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \overline{L}^0_u + \frac{2K\varepsilon}{\Delta t}.
\tag{5.6}

Proof. If  \sum c_u > 0 , extending  s  beyond one period adds a positive quantity to (5.5), so  m_t \to \infty  and no finite periodic solution exists. If  \sum c_u \le 0 , the maximum in (5.5) is attained within one period, and  m_t  is finite and periodic.  \square 

The orbit balance is the correct precondition: the average generation must exceed the average auxiliary load plus the estimation-error drift. The condition  \overline{L} - \underline{P} > 0  of v1.1 is neither necessary nor sufficient.

5.4 Invariance and Recursive Feasibility

Proposition 5.2 (Invariance). Let  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t . Then the zero-compute action satisfies

\mathcal{R}_t \subseteq [0, \infty) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \hat{B}_{t+1} \ge m_{t+1}.
\tag{5.7}

Proof. Since  m_t \ge a_t , we have  \hat{B}_t - \varepsilon + \Delta t(\underline{P}_t - \overline{L}^0_t) \ge \hat{B}_t - a_t \ge 0 , so  \mathcal{R}_t \subseteq [0, \infty) . The estimate update gives  \hat{B}_{t+1} \ge \hat{B}_t - c_t \ge m_t - c_t \ge m_{t+1} , where the last inequality is the recursion (5.4).  \square 

Corollary 5.3 (Recursive feasibility). If  \hat{B}_0 \ge m_0 , then  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t  for all  t \ge 0  under the zero-compute action, and the margin filter

\Phi^\circ_t(\hat{B}_t) = \bigl\{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}_t) : (\mathcal{R}_t \pm \varepsilon) \subseteq [m_{t+1}, \infty)\bigr\}
\tag{5.8}

is non-empty at every step. The  \pm \varepsilon  is the correction that v1.2's  \Phi^\circ  was missing.

Corollary 5.4 (Upper saturation). With the shunt  \min(B^{\max}, \cdot) , the true state satisfies  B_t \le B^{\max}  always, and the projected estimate satisfies  \hat{B}_t \le B^{\max} . The margin is one-sided:  \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t) = [m_t, B^{\max}] .

5.5 Non-Emptiness and Eclipse Example

The margin is non-empty when

B^{\max} \ge \max_t m_t.
\tag{5.9}

For an eclipse of  n_e  steps at constant load  L , with the orbit balance holding:

c_t = 2\varepsilon + \Delta t L, \qquad a_t = \varepsilon + \Delta t L,
\tag{5.10}

and the maximum in (5.5) is attained at the last eclipse step:

m_0 = a_0 + (n_e - 1) c_0 = n_e \Delta t L + (2n_e - 1)\varepsilon.
\tag{5.11}

Hence

B^{\max} \ge n_e \Delta t L + (2n_e - 1)\varepsilon.
\tag{5.12}

The  2n_e - 1  factor is the correction over v1.1's  2\varepsilon  per eclipse: the estimate can drift downward by  c_t  at each of  n_e - 1  interior steps, and the final step needs only the one-step safety bound  a_t .

5.6 Relation to the Constant Margin of v1.1

The constant margin  \varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P})  is the special case  c \le 0 , i.e.,  \Delta t(\underline{P} - \overline{L}) \ge 2\varepsilon . This is the condition under which the estimate cannot drift downward, so the margin is time-invariant. v1.1 did not state this condition. When  c > 0 , the estimate drifts downward, the margin is time-indexed, and the constant form is invalid. This formalizes why  \overline{L} - \underline{P} > 0  was the wrong precondition: the correct precondition is the orbit balance (5.6).

5.7 Estimator-Derived Error Bound

For the set-membership observer with sensor noise  |v_B| \le v_B  and reset at each step,

\varepsilon_B^\infty = 2 v_B + \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P}).
\tag{5.13}

Substituting into (5.6) and (5.12) gives explicit design inequalities in terms of sensor noise, disturbance bounds, and  \Delta t .

