7. Coupled Margin and Common-Action Characterization

7.1 Coupled Margin

\mathcal{S}^\circ = \bigl\{(\hat{B}, \hat{T}) : \exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}),\ \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t),\ \hat{T} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_T(a),\ \mathcal{R}_B(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t+1),\ \mathcal{R}_T(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_T(a)\bigr\}.
\tag{7.1}

Proposition 7.1. If the common-action condition holds at every  \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ , then  \mathcal{S}^\circ  is controlled-invariant.

Proof. The successor lies in both margins, hence in their intersection.  \square 

The common-action condition is the deliverable; it is not automatic. The energy margin is one-sided and time-indexed; the thermal margin is action-dependent and time-invariant.

7.2 Common-Action Failure Set

Let  \mathcal{F}  be the set of  (\hat{B}, \hat{T}) \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t) \times \mathcal{S}^\circ_T  for which the admissible action set partitions into  \mathcal{A}_B  (energy-safe, thermal-unsafe) and  \mathcal{A}_T  (thermal-safe, energy-unsafe) with empty intersection. Then  \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset  certifies that no common action exists for the declared uncertainty, and the physical regime is one of genuine conflict: the action that keeps storage safe overheats the node, and the action that keeps the node cool depletes storage.

