8. Interval Arithmetic and Validator Soundness

The validator evaluates an interval enclosure  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) .

Lemma 8.1. If  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a)  is computed by interval arithmetic over  \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times [W] , then  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) .

Lemma 8.2. If the validator accepts  a  only when  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} , then every accepted action satisfies  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} .

The closed-graph property of  \Phi  is an upper-semicontinuity statement about convergent sequences; it does not imply that an action safe at  \hat{x}  remains safe at a nearby  \hat{x}' . Interval-arithmetic soundness is the correct justification for floating-point implementation.

