9. Schedulability: T2 Resolved

9.1 Restricted Model

Single node, deterministic generation  P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t) , no thermal coupling, preemptive scheduling, constant power during execution, instantaneous power limits satisfied at every instant. Task system  \tau = \{(r_j, d_j, e_j)\} .

9.2 Corrected ED-H Test

The demand bound over  [t_1, t_2]  is

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) = \sum_{j : r_j \ge t_1,\ d_j \le t_2} e_j.
\tag{9.1}

The test requires, for every interval,

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) - B^{\min},
\tag{9.2}

and the processor-demand condition

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) \le t_2 - t_1.
\tag{9.3}

Remark 9.1 (Saturation cap removed). v1.2 imposed  \int P^{\mathrm{gen}} + B(t_1) \le B^{\max}  as a feasibility constraint. As written, this declared a task set infeasible exactly when energy was plentiful, which is backwards. The correct role of  B^{\max}  is to limit usable energy when curtailment occurs. The usable energy over  [t_1, t_2]  is

E_{\mathrm{usable}}(t_1, t_2) = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) - B(t_2) - \mathrm{curtailment}(t_1, t_2),
\tag{9.4}

and the test (9.2) is exact only when curtailment is zero. When curtailment occurs, (9.2) is necessary but not sufficient, and sufficiency requires a schedule that minimizes curtailment. The term  B(t_1)  is defined by the schedule, not assumed given.

9.3 Necessity and Sufficiency

Theorem 9.2. Under the restricted model with zero curtailment, the ED-H test (9.2)–(9.3) is necessary and sufficient for feasibility.

Proof sketch. Necessity: any feasible schedule satisfies the demand bound and the processor-demand condition over every interval. Sufficiency: the ED-H schedule is feasible if both hold; the proof constructs the schedule and verifies deadlines.  \square 

Theorem 9.3 (Zero duality gap). For the restricted single-node preemptive model with zero curtailment, the Lagrangian dual has zero duality gap.

Proof sketch. The feasible set is a polymatroid intersection; the preemptive, constant-power assumptions make the LP relaxation integral.  \square 

The general multi-node coupled case is not expected to have zero duality gap.

