10. Online Performance: T3

10.1 Drift-Plus-Penalty

Virtual queues:

Q_{B_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{B_i}(t) - B_i(t) + B_i^{\min}, 0\bigr] + \bigl[B_i^{\max} - B_i(t)\bigr]_+,
\tag{10.1}

Q_{T_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{T_i}(t) - (T_i^{\max} - T_i(t)), 0\bigr] + \bigl[T_i(t) - T_i^{\max}\bigr]_+.
\tag{10.2}

Remark 10.1 (Vacuous thermal queue). Under the filter,  T_i(t) \le T_i^{\max}  at every step, so  [T_i(t) - T_i^{\max}]_+ \equiv 0  and the thermal queue (10.2) provides no useful drift gradient. It should be replaced by a soft thermal constraint or removed from the analysis.

Remark 10.2 (Mean storage constraint). The storage queue (10.1) enforces only  \mathbb{E}[B_i] \ge (B_i^{\max} + B_i^{\min})/2  in the mean-rate sense. It does not enforce  B_i(t) \ge 0  at every instant; that is the filter's role.

10.2 Bound and Comparator

The drift-plus-penalty algorithm minimizes  \Delta(t) + V\,\mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t) \mid Q(t)] . The time-average bound is

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t)] \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V},
\tag{10.3}

with  \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}}  the optimum over stationary policies satisfying the same constraints.

Remark 10.3 (Comparator assumptions). The comparator  \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}}  requires the generation process to be i.i.d. or ergodic. The adversarial disturbance set  W  of Section 2.3 is not ergodic, so the bound (10.3) does not apply to the worst-case disturbance. The correct comparator for adversarial  W  is the best stationary policy for the empirical distribution, and the bound holds only in expectation over that distribution. This is stated as a limitation.

Remark 10.4 (Slater slack). The  O(V)  backlog bound requires a Slater condition: there exists a stationary policy with strictly feasible constraints, i.e., a slack  \sigma > 0  in the mean constraints. Without slack, the backlog may grow without bound even under a stationary feasible policy.

10.3 Coupled Case

For the coupled problem, the bound (10.3) is not derived. The coupling introduces a joint constraint that may not admit a stationary policy satisfying both energy and thermal constraints simultaneously. The thesis reports the decoupled bound and states the coupled case open.

