11. Fault Tolerance: T4

11.1 Independent Failure Model (Corrected)

Each node fails independently at each step with probability  p , and recovers after a geometric time with mean  1/q . The parameter  q = 0  is the no-recovery case and  q = \infty  is instantaneous recovery. The completion probability for a critical task of duration  d  with redundancy  r  satisfies the recursion

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1-p)^d + \sum_{k=1}^{d} p(1-p)^{k-1} P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r-1, d-k-\delta),
\tag{11.1}

with base case

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } d \le 0 \text{ and } r \ge 1, \\ 0 & \text{if } d > 0 \text{ and } r = 0. \end{cases}
\tag{11.2}

The handoff delay is  \delta \approx 1/q  for  q > 0 . For  q = 0  (no recovery), the recursion collapses to

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = 1 - \bigl[1 - (1-p)^d\bigr]^r.
\tag{11.3}

v1.2 labeled (11.3) as the  q = \infty  case; it is the  q = 0  case.

11.2 Correlated Failure Model (Transient Analysis)

The two-state Markov chain has states  \{\text{nominal}, \text{degraded}\}  with transition probabilities  p_{01}  (nominal to degraded) and  p_{10}  (degraded to nominal). The stationary probabilities are

\pi_{\mathrm{nom}} = \frac{p_{10}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}, \qquad \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} = \frac{p_{01}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}.
\tag{11.4}

Remark 11.1 (Stationary weights are incorrect for finite-duration tasks). v1.2 used  \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} P_{\mathrm{ind}} + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} P_{\mathrm{deg}}  as if the chain were frozen for the whole task. The correct treatment is a transient analysis. The probability of no degraded entry during  d  steps is  (1 - p_{01})^d , and any degraded interval that overlaps the task kills all replicas simultaneously. Hence

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1 - p_{01})^d \, P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, d, p_0),
\tag{11.5}

where  P_{\mathrm{ind}}  is the independent formula with nominal failure probability  p_0 . If the degraded state is recoverable with Weibull holding time  F_{\mathrm{deg}}(t) = 1 - e^{-(t/\lambda)^k} , a task can survive a degraded interval only if the interval ends before the task's critical phase; the general form is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \sum_{k=0}^{d} P(\text{stay nominal for } k \text{ steps}) \cdot P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, k, p_0) \cdot P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d-k),
\tag{11.6}

solved numerically. For a task requiring at least one node throughout,  P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d-k) = 0  for  d-k > 0 , and (11.5) is exact.

11.3 Redundancy Threshold

For prescribed  \alpha ,  r^*(\alpha, d, p, \delta)  is the smallest  r  with  P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) \ge 1 - \alpha , computed by binary search.

