14. Conclusion

STAR-BRICK 01 v1.3 repairs the most serious gaps of v1.2. The energy margin is now time-indexed, derived by backward recursion over the orbit, with a necessary and sufficient orbit-balance condition and a proof of invariance and recursive feasibility. The thermal margin evaluates the cooling condition at the margin boundary rather than at  T^{\max} . The ED-H saturation cap is removed from the feasibility conditions and replaced by a curtailment-aware usable-energy term; the processor-demand condition is restored. Faults are placed in the disturbance set  W , resolving the conflict with exact discrete observation. The fault-tolerance model is corrected for the  q = 0  versus  q = \infty  labels, the base case, and the transient analysis of the correlated state. The Lyapunov queues are qualified, and the comparator is restricted to ergodic generation. The theorem ledger records the resolved and open obligations. No flight readiness, no physical safety beyond the model, and no benefit from LLM assistance without experimental evidence are claimed.

The first executable artifact tests Theorem 1 and the time-indexed margin: it constructs the smallest state space, exhibits the recursive-feasibility failure of the constant margin, and demonstrates the time-indexed margin restoring feasibility with the predicted eclipse bound (5.12). Only after that artifact behaves as the theorem predicts does the work extend to the corrected ED-H test, the corrected fault-tolerance model, and the coupled-margin experiment.

