Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 State Space and Metric

The state space is  X = X^c \times X^d , where

X^c \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_c}, \qquad X^d = \{0,1\}^N \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{G},

with  X^c  containing stored energy  B \in \mathbb{R}^N , temperature  T \in \mathbb{R}^N , and computing capacity  C \in \mathbb{R}^N , and  X^d  containing availability  A \in \{0,1\}^N , task queue  Q \in \mathcal{Q} , and communication graph  \mathcal{N} \in \mathcal{G} . The metric is

d(x, \hat{x}) = \begin{cases} \|x^c - \hat{x}^c\|_2 & \text{if } x^d = \hat{x}^d, \\ +\infty & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases} \tag{A.1}

The metric is symmetric, vanishes only at  x = \hat{x} , and satisfies the triangle inequality on each branch. It is not induced by any norm on  X , because the discrete component does not embed in a Euclidean space in a physically meaningful way.

The estimation hypotheses are

x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, \qquad \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon \ge 0. \tag{A.2}

The discrete component is observed exactly. The continuous component is observed through an estimate with radius  \varepsilon . Both are hypotheses of the safety theorem, not consequences.

A.2 Regularity Assumptions

Assumption A.1 (Continuity). The map  f^c : X^c \times X^d \times U \times W \to X^c , obtained by projecting  f  onto its continuous component, is continuous on its domain.

Assumption A.2 (Compactness).  W \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m  is compact. For every  \hat{x} \in X , the robust admissible set  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x})  is compact.

Assumption A.3 (Closed graph of  \hat{\mathcal{A}} ). The set  \{(\hat{x}, a) : a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x})\}  is closed in  X \times U .

Assumption A.4 (Disturbance process).  (w_t)_{t \ge 0}  is adapted to a filtration  (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t \ge 0}  on a probability space  (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) , with  w_t \in W  almost surely for all  t .

Under A.1–A.3, the reachable set

\mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) = \bigl\{f(x, a, w) : x^c \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c),\ x^d = \hat{x}^d,\ w \in W\bigr\} \tag{A.3}

is compact for every  (\hat{x}, a) , because it is the continuous image of the compact set  \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times \{a\} \times W .

A.3 Robust Admissible Set

Because the controller uses  \hat{x}_t , not  x_t , admissibility is checked against the estimate:

\hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in U : a \in \mathcal{A}(x) \ \forall x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\}\bigr\}. \tag{A.4}

It is generally smaller than  \mathcal{A}(\hat{x}) , and it can be empty. When  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \emptyset , no action is robustly admissible, and the system enters the safe state. The construction of  \hat{\mathcal{A}}  is a deliverable of the estimator design.

A.4 Interval Arithmetic

An interval is  [a] = [\underline{a}, \overline{a}]  with  \underline{a} \le \overline{a} . The fundamental theorem of interval arithmetic states: if  g : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}  is a rational function and  [x] \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n  is an interval box, then

g([x]) \supseteq \{g(x) : x \in [x]\}. \tag{A.5}

This is the inclusion property. The mean value form

g(x) \in g(m) + g'([x])([x] - m), \qquad m \in [x], \tag{A.6}

reduces the over-approximation from  O(\text{width})  to  O(\text{width}^2)  for differentiable  g . The dependency problem — repeated variables treated independently — is the principal source of over-approximation. For the storage dynamics,  B_t  appears once on the right-hand side, so the dependency problem is mild. For the thermal dynamics,  T_t  appears in both linear and quartic cooling terms, so the mean value form is used.

A.5 Viability Kernels

The viability kernel is defined by the iterated operator

\mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}, \qquad \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigl\{x \in \mathcal{S} : \exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x),\ \forall w \in W,\ f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})\bigr\}, \tag{A.7}

with  \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_{k \ge 0} \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) .

Lemma A.5. Under Assumptions A.1–A.3, each  \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  is closed, and  \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S})  is closed.

Proof. Induction on  k . For  k = 0 ,  \mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}  is closed by hypothesis. Suppose  \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  is closed. Let  (x_j)  be a sequence in  \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S})  with  x_j \to x . For each  j , there exists  a_j \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_j)  with  f(x_j, a_j, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  for every  w . By compactness of  \hat{\mathcal{A}}  in a neighborhood of  x , the sequence  (a_j)  has a convergent subsequence  a_{j_\ell} \to a . By Assumption A.3,  a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x) . By continuity of  f  and closedness of  \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) ,  f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  for every  w . Hence  x \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) , which is closed. The intersection of closed sets is closed.  \square 

Lemma A.6 (Maximal invariant subset). If  x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) , there exists a policy  \pi  such that  x_t \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S})  for all  t \ge 0 .

Proof. Suppose  x_0 \in \bigcap_k \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) . Then  x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S})  for every  k . By definition, for each  k  there exists  a_0^{(k)} \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0)  with  f(x_0, a_0^{(k)}, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  for every  w \in W . By compactness of  \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0) , the sequence  (a_0^{(k)})  has a convergent subsequence with limit  a_0 \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0) . By continuity of  f  and closedness of each  \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) ,  f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  for every  k  and every  w , hence  f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) . The induction closes at each subsequent step.  \square 

A.6 Discrete-Time Control Barrier Functions

A function  h : X^c \to \mathbb{R}  is a discrete-time control barrier function for the set  \mathcal{S}_h = \{x^c : h(x^c) \ge 0\}  if there exists  \gamma \in (0,1]  such that for every  x^c \in \mathcal{S}_h , there exists  a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x)  with

h(f^c(x, a, w)) \ge (1 - \gamma) h(x^c) \qquad \forall w \in W. \tag{A.8}

Lemma A.7. If  h  is a discrete-time CBF and  h(x_0^c) \ge 0 , then  h(x_t^c) \ge 0  for all  t \ge 0  under any policy that selects a CBF-satisfying action at each step.

Proof. By induction. If  h(x_t^c) \ge 0 , then  h(x_{t+1}^c) \ge (1-\gamma) h(x_t^c) \ge 0 .  \square 

The CBF condition is sufficient for forward invariance; it is not necessary. Smaller  \gamma  admits more actions but recovers more slowly from near the boundary.

