Appendix B — Proofs of Safety and Viability Results

B.1 Theorem 1 (Soundness of the One-Step Safety Filter)

Theorem 1. Let Assumptions A.1–A.4 hold. Suppose  x_0 \in \mathcal{S} , and for every  t \ge 0 :  x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d ,  \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon ,  w_t \in W  almost surely, and the controller selects  a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t) . Then

\mathbb{P}\bigl[\forall t \ge 0,\ x_t \in \mathcal{S}\bigr] = 1. \tag{B.1}

Proof. Let  \Omega_W = \bigcap_{t \ge 0} \{w_t \in W\} . By Assumption A.4,  \mathbb{P}[\Omega_W] = 1 . Fix  \omega \in \Omega_W . The proof is an induction on  t  along  \omega .

Base.  x_0 \in \mathcal{S}  by hypothesis.

Step. Suppose  x_t \in \mathcal{S} ,  x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d , and  \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon . Then  x_t \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}_t^c) \times \{\hat{x}_t^d\} . Since  a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t) , the definition of  \Phi  gives  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S} . Since  w_t \in W ,

x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}. \tag{B.2}

The induction closes for every  \omega \in \Omega_W .  \square 

Remark B.1.1 (Almost-sure, not deterministic). The conclusion is almost-sure because the hypothesis on  w_t  is almost-sure. If  w_t \in W  holds for every sample path, the conclusion holds for every sample path. The validator enforces the deterministic version when the disturbance set is treated as a hard bound.

Remark B.1.2 (Conditionality). The theorem is conditional on three hypotheses: the continuous estimation-error bound holds at every step, the discrete state is observed exactly, and the filter is non-empty at every step. None is derived. The first is a property of the estimator; the second is a property of the sensing and communication layer; the third is recursive feasibility.

Remark B.1.3 (Relative soundness). Soundness is with respect to  f ,  \mathcal{S} , and  W . If the physical system deviates from  f , or if  \mathcal{S}  omits a hazard, the theorem does not protect against it. Numerical rounding is enclosed by evaluating  \mathcal{R}  in interval arithmetic (Appendix C). Physical model error is addressed by model validation.

B.2 Proposition 4.2 (Margin Inflation)

Proposition 4.2. Let  \mathcal{S}^\circ  be a candidate margin, and suppose every  \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ  admits a common action  a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x})  with  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ . Then  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) .

Proof. Under the hypothesis,  \mathcal{S}^\circ  is controlled-invariant. We show by induction on  k  that  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})  for every  k . For  k = 0 ,  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathcal{S}  since the margin is a subset of the safe set. Suppose  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) . For  \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ , the common action  a  satisfies  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) . Hence  \hat{x} \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) . Therefore  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) . Taking the intersection over  k ,  \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) .  \square 

B.3 Proposition 5.1 (Orbit Balance)

Proposition 5.1. A finite periodic solution to the backward recursion

m_t = \max\bigl(a_t,\ m_{t+1} + c_t\bigr) \tag{B.3}

exists if and only if  \sum_{u=0}^{K-1} c_u \le 0 .

Proof. Let  S = \sum_{u=t}^{t+K-1} c_u  and  F = \max_{s \in [t, t+K-1]} [a_s + \sum_{u=t}^{s-1} c_u] . Unrolling (B.3)  K  times and imposing periodicity  m_{t+K} = m_t ,

m_t = \max\bigl(F,\ m_t + S\bigr). \tag{B.4}

If  S > 0 :  m_t + S > m_t , so the right side exceeds  m_t  for every finite  m_t ; the only solution is  m_t = +\infty . No finite solution exists.

If  S \le 0 : the unique least solution is  m_t = F . To see this: if  m_t = F , then  \max(F, F + S) = F  because  F + S \le F . Conversely, if  m_t < F , then  \max(F, m_t + S) \ge F > m_t , so the equation fails. Hence the least solution is  m_t = F .  \square 

Substituting  a_s = \varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L}^0_s - \underline{P}_s)  and  c_u = 2\varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L}^0_u - \underline{P}_u) , the orbit balance  S \le 0  becomes

\sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \underline{P}_u \ge \sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \overline{L}^0_u + \frac{2K\varepsilon}{\Delta t}. \tag{B.5}

B.4 Proposition 5.2 (Energy Invariance)

Proposition 5.2. Let  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t . Then under the zero-compute action,

\mathcal{R}_t \subseteq [0, \infty) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \hat{B}_{t+1} \ge m_{t+1}. \tag{B.6}

Proof. Since  m_t \ge a_t = \varepsilon + \Delta t(\overline{L}^0_t - \underline{P}_t) ,

\hat{B}_t - \varepsilon + \Delta t(\underline{P}_t - \overline{L}^0_t) \ge \hat{B}_t - a_t \ge 0. \tag{B.7}

The lower endpoint of  \mathcal{R}_t  is  \hat{B}_t - \varepsilon + \Delta t(\underline{P}_t - \overline{L}^0_t) \ge 0 , so  \mathcal{R}_t \subseteq [0, \infty) .

For the estimate update,  \hat{B}_{t+1} \ge \hat{B}_t - c_t . Since  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t  and  m_t \ge m_{t+1} + c_t  by (B.3),

\hat{B}_{t+1} \ge \hat{B}_t - c_t \ge m_t - c_t \ge m_{t+1}. \tag{B.8}

 \square 

B.5 Corollary 5.3 (Recursive Feasibility)

Corollary 5.3. If  \hat{B}_0 \ge m_0 , then  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t  for all  t \ge 0  under the zero-compute action, and the margin filter

\Phi^\circ_t(\hat{B}_t) = \bigl\{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}_t) : (\mathcal{R}_t \pm \varepsilon) \subseteq [m_{t+1}, \infty)\bigr\} \tag{B.9}

is non-empty at every step.

Proof. The estimate bound  \hat{B}_t \ge m_t  follows by induction from Proposition 5.2. The zero-compute action belongs to  \Phi^\circ_t(\hat{B}_t)  by Proposition 5.2, so the filter is non-empty.  \square 

B.6 Corollary 5.4 (Upper Saturation)

Corollary 5.4. With the shunt  B_{t+1} = \min(B^{\max}, B_t + \Delta t(P_t - L_t)) , the true state satisfies  B_t \le B^{\max}  always, and the projected estimate satisfies  \hat{B}_t \le B^{\max} . The margin is one-sided:  \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t) = [m_t, B^{\max}] .

Proof. The first claim is immediate from the shunt. The estimate satisfies  \hat{B}_t \le B_t + \varepsilon  and  B_t \le B^{\max} ; projecting  \hat{B}_t  onto  [0, B^{\max}]  gives  \hat{B}_t \le B^{\max} . The margin is  [m_t, B^{\max}]  because the lower bound is  m_t  and the upper bound is enforced by the shunt.  \square 

B.7 Set-Membership Estimator

Let  X_t  be the set of states consistent with measurements up to time  t , with diameter  \varepsilon_t .

Prediction. Under the storage dynamics  B_{t+1} = B_t + \Delta t(P_t - L_t)  with  P_t \in [\underline{P}, \overline{P}]  and  L_t \in [0, \overline{L}] ,

X_{t+1|t} = X_t + \Delta t[\underline{P} - \overline{L}, \overline{P}], \qquad \mathrm{diam}(X_{t+1|t}) = \varepsilon_t + \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P} + \overline{L}). \tag{B.10}

Measurement update. With  y_t = B_t + v_t ,  |v_t| \le v_B ,

X_{t+1} = X_{t+1|t} \cap [y_{t+1} - v_B,\ y_{t+1} + v_B], \qquad \mathrm{diam}(X_{t+1}) \le \min\bigl(\mathrm{diam}(X_{t+1|t}),\ 2v_B\bigr). \tag{B.11}

Proposition B.7.1. The estimation error satisfies the recursion

\varepsilon_{t+1} \le \min\bigl(\varepsilon_t + \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P} + \overline{L}),\ 2v_B\bigr). \tag{B.12}

The least fixed point is  \varepsilon = 2v_B  when  \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P} + \overline{L}) > 0 , and  \varepsilon = 2v_B  otherwise. If the estimate is taken as the midpoint of  X_t , the error bound is  \varepsilon = v_B .

Proof. Immediate from (B.10)–(B.11). The fixed point satisfies  \varepsilon = \min(\varepsilon + \alpha, 2v_B)  with  \alpha = \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P} + \overline{L}) . If  \varepsilon < 2v_B  and  \alpha > 0 , then  \varepsilon + \alpha > \varepsilon , so the minimum is either  \varepsilon + \alpha  or  2v_B ; the equation  \varepsilon = \varepsilon + \alpha  fails, so  \varepsilon = 2v_B . If  \varepsilon \ge 2v_B , the minimum is  2v_B , so  \varepsilon = 2v_B . The midpoint estimate has error at most  \mathrm{diam}(X_t)/2 \le v_B .  \square 

B.8 Proposition 6.1 (Thermal Invariance)

Proposition 6.1. Suppose  Q^{\mathrm{out}}  is non-decreasing on  [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]  and

Q^{\mathrm{out}}\bigl(T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon\bigr) \ge Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) + \frac{C^{\mathrm{th}}\, \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}}{\Delta t}. \tag{B.13}

Then  \mathcal{S}^\circ_T(a) = \{\hat{T} : \hat{T} \le T^\circ(a)\}  with

T^\circ(a) = T^{\max} - 2\varepsilon - \frac{\Delta t\, Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a)}{C^{\mathrm{th}}} - \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}} \tag{B.14}

is controlled-invariant under action  a .

Proof. Let  \hat{T}_t \le T^\circ(a) , so the true  T_t \le T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon . The successor satisfies

T_{t+1} = T_t + \frac{\Delta t}{C^{\mathrm{th}}}\bigl(Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T_t)\bigr) + e_{\mathrm{disc}}. \tag{B.15}

Since  Q^{\mathrm{out}}  is non-decreasing and  T_t \le T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon ,

Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T_t) \le Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon) \le Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon). \tag{B.16}

Using (B.13),

Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T_t) \le Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon) \le -\frac{C^{\mathrm{th}}\, \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}}{\Delta t}. \tag{B.17}

Hence

T_{t+1} \le T_t - \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}} + e_{\mathrm{disc}} \le T_t \le T^\circ(a) + \varepsilon. \tag{B.18}

The next estimate satisfies  \hat{T}_{t+1} \le T_{t+1} + \varepsilon \le T^\circ(a) + 2\varepsilon . The margin (B.14) is defined with  2\varepsilon , so  \hat{T}_{t+1} \le T^\circ(a) .  \square 

B.9 Proposition 7.1 (Coupled Invariance)

Proposition 7.1. If the common-action condition holds at every  \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ , then  \mathcal{S}^\circ  is controlled-invariant.

Proof. The common action is the hypothesis. Under it,  \mathcal{R}_B(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t+1)  and  \mathcal{R}_T(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_T(a) . Hence  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B(t+1) \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T(a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ , so  \mathcal{S}^\circ  is controlled-invariant.  \square 

B.10 Discretization Error Bounds

Lemma B.10.1. For the explicit Euler scheme  T_{t+1} = T_t + \Delta t\, g(T_t)  applied to  \dot{T} = g(T)  with  g \in C^2 ,

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} |g'(T)\, g(T)|. \tag{B.19}

Proof. Taylor expansion of the exact solution:

T(t + \Delta t) = T(t) + \Delta t\, g(T(t)) + \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \ddot{T}(t) + O(\Delta t^3), \qquad \ddot{T} = g'(T)\, g(T). \tag{B.20}

Subtracting the Euler step yields the bound. The units are temperature.  \square 

Lemma B.10.2 (Equivalent form). With  g(T) = (Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T))/C^{\mathrm{th}} ,

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2 (C^{\mathrm{th}})^2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} \left| \frac{dQ^{\mathrm{out}}}{dT}(T) \cdot \bigl(Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T)\bigr) \right|. \tag{B.21}

Proof. Compute  g'(T) = -Q'^{\mathrm{out}}(T)/C^{\mathrm{th}}  and  g(T) = (Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T))/C^{\mathrm{th}} . Substituting into  g'(T) g(T)  yields the stated expression.  \square 

Lemma B.10.3 (Stability). For the linearized cooling dynamics  \dot{T} = -(T - T_{\mathrm{env}})/\tau  with  \tau = C^{\mathrm{th}}/h , the explicit Euler scheme is stable if  \Delta t < 2\tau .

Proof. The amplification factor is  1 - \Delta t/\tau . Stability requires  |1 - \Delta t/\tau| \le 1 , which gives  0 \le \Delta t/\tau \le 2 .  \square 

