Appendix C — Interval Arithmetic and Validator Soundness

C.1 Interval Inclusion

Lemma C.1. If  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a)  is computed by interval arithmetic over the box  \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times [W] , then  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) .

Proof. Direct from the inclusion property (A.5), applied componentwise.  \square 

Lemma C.2. If the validator accepts  a  only when  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} , then every accepted action satisfies  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} .

Proof. By Lemma C.1,  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) . If  [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} , then  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} .  \square 

Remark C.3. The closed-graph property of  \Phi  is an upper-semicontinuity statement: if  (\hat{x}_k, a_k) \to (\hat{x}, a)  with  a_k \in \Phi(\hat{x}_k) , then  a \in \Phi(\hat{x}) . It does not say that if  a \in \Phi(\hat{x})  and  \hat{x}'  is close to  \hat{x} , then  a \in \Phi(\hat{x}') . The latter is false in general. The correct justification for floating-point implementation is Lemma C.2.

C.2 Dependency Problem

The interval evaluation of  f  over the box  \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times [W]  may over-approximate  \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a)  due to the dependency problem. For the storage dynamics,  B_t  appears once, so the over-approximation is  O(\varepsilon) . For the thermal dynamics,  T_t  appears in both linear and quartic terms; the mean value form (A.6) reduces the over-approximation to  O(\varepsilon^2) . The validator's acceptance condition is therefore conservative: it may reject an action that is safe under exact arithmetic, but it never accepts an action that is unsafe.

