Appendix D — Schedulability Proofs

D.1 Necessity and Sufficiency of ED-H

Theorem 9.2. Under the restricted model (single node, deterministic generation, no thermal coupling, preemptive scheduling, constant power, zero curtailment), the ED-H test (9.2)–(9.3) is necessary and sufficient for feasibility.

Proof.

Necessity. Let  \sigma  be a feasible schedule. For any interval  [t_1, t_2] , the total execution of tasks with  r_j \ge t_1  and  d_j \le t_2  must be completed within  [t_1, t_2] . The energy consumed is at least  \mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) , and it must be supplied by generation plus initial storage minus final storage:

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) - B(t_2) \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) - B^{\min}. \tag{D.1}

The processor-demand condition  \mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) \le t_2 - t_1  is necessary because a single preemptive processor cannot execute more than  t_2 - t_1  units of work in  [t_1, t_2] .

Sufficiency. Construct the ED-H schedule: at each time, execute the task with the earliest deadline among those available, subject to the energy constraint. Suppose the schedule misses a deadline for task  j  at time  d_j . Let  t_1  be the latest time before  d_j  at which the processor was idle or executing a task with deadline later than  d_j . Then in  [t_1, d_j] , the processor is continuously busy with tasks of deadline  \le d_j , so the total execution in  [t_1, d_j]  equals  d_j - t_1 . By the processor-demand condition,  \mathrm{dbf}(t_1, d_j) \le d_j - t_1 , and by the energy condition, the demand is met. This contradicts the missed deadline. Hence the schedule is feasible.  \square 

D.2 Zero Duality Gap

Theorem 9.3. For the restricted single-node preemptive model with zero curtailment, the Lagrangian dual has zero duality gap.

Proof sketch. The feasible set of the scheduling problem is a polymatroid: the preemptive, constant-power assumptions make the set of feasible execution profiles a polymatroid with rank function  \mathrm{dbf} . The energy constraint is a linear polymatroid constraint. By the polymatroid intersection theorem, the LP relaxation is integral, so the integer optimum equals the LP optimum. Weak duality gives dual optimum  \le  primal optimum, and integrality gives primal optimum  =  LP optimum  \le  dual optimum. Hence the gap is zero.  \square 

D.3 Curtailment and the Corrected Saturation Cap

When curtailment occurs, the usable energy over  [t_1, t_2]  is

E_{\mathrm{usable}}(t_1, t_2) = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) - B(t_2) - \mathrm{curtailment}(t_1, t_2). \tag{D.2}

The test (9.2) is exact only when curtailment is zero. When curtailment is positive, (9.2) is necessary but not sufficient, and sufficiency requires a schedule that minimizes curtailment. The saturation cap of v1.2,  \int P^{\mathrm{gen}} + B(t_1) \le B^{\max} , is not a feasibility constraint; it is a modeling constraint that determines the curtailment term.

