Appendix E — Online Performance Proofs

E.1 Lyapunov Drift

Let  Q(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^k  be virtual queues with

Q_i(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_i(t) - b_i(t), 0\bigr] + a_i(t). \tag{E.1}

The quadratic Lyapunov function is  L(t) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_i Q_i(t)^2 , and the one-step drift is  \Delta(t) = \mathbb{E}[L(t+1) - L(t) \mid Q(t)] .

Lemma E.1 (Drift bound).

\Delta(t) \le B + \sum_i Q_i(t)\, \mathbb{E}\bigl[a_i(t) - b_i(t) \mid Q(t)\bigr], \qquad B = \frac{1}{2} \sum_i \bigl(a_i^{\max 2} + b_i^{\max 2}\bigr). \tag{E.2}

Proof. Standard. See Neely (2010), Theorem 4.1.  \square 

E.2 Drift-Plus-Penalty

The drift-plus-penalty algorithm minimizes  \Delta(t) + V\, \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t) \mid Q(t)] .

Theorem E.2. The time-average penalty satisfies

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t)] \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V}, \tag{E.3}

with  \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}}  the optimum over stationary policies satisfying the same constraints.

Proof. Standard. See Neely (2010), Theorem 4.5.  \square 

E.3 Slater Condition and  O(V)  Backlog

The  O(V)  backlog bound requires a Slater condition: there exists a stationary policy with strictly feasible constraints, i.e., a slack  \sigma > 0  in the mean constraints. Without slack, the backlog may grow without bound even under a stationary feasible policy. The bound is  \mathbb{E}[Q_i] \le O(V)  for the storage and thermal queues.

E.4 Comparator Qualification

The comparator  \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}}  requires the generation process to be i.i.d. or ergodic. The adversarial disturbance set  W  of Section 2.3 is not ergodic, so the bound (E.3) does not apply to the worst-case disturbance. The correct comparator for adversarial  W  is the best stationary policy for the empirical distribution, and the bound holds only in expectation over that distribution.

