Appendix F — Fault Tolerance Proofs

F.1 Independent Failure Recursion

Proposition F.1. For a critical task of duration  d  with redundancy  r , independent per-step failure probability  p , and handoff delay  \delta , the completion probability satisfies

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1-p)^d + \sum_{k=1}^{d} p(1-p)^{k-1} P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r-1, d-k-\delta), \tag{F.1}

with base case

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } d \le 0 \text{ and } r \ge 1, \\ 0 & \text{if } d > 0 \text{ and } r = 0. \end{cases} \tag{F.2}

Proof. Condition on the first step. With probability  (1-p) , no node fails in the first step, and the task proceeds with duration  d - 1  and redundancy  r . With probability  p , one node fails at step  k  (for some  k \in \{1, \ldots, d\} ), and the task is handed off to the remaining  r - 1  replicas after delay  \delta , with remaining duration  d - k - \delta . The recursion follows by summing over  k . The base case: a task of non-positive duration completes with any positive redundancy; a task of positive duration with zero redundancy cannot complete.  \square 

Proposition F.2 (No-recovery case,  q = 0 ). If recovery is impossible,  P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = 1 - [1 - (1-p)^d]^r .

Proof. Each replica completes the task iff it does not fail in any of the  d  steps, with probability  (1-p)^d . The  r  replicas are independent, so at least one completes with probability  1 - [1 - (1-p)^d]^r .  \square 

Remark F.3. v1.2 labeled (F.2) as the  q = \infty  case. It is the  q = 0  case. Instantaneous recovery ( q = \infty ) corresponds to  \delta = 0  in the recursion (F.1).

F.2 Correlated Failure: Transient Analysis

Proposition F.4. Let the two-state Markov chain have transition probabilities  p_{01}  (nominal to degraded) and  p_{10}  (degraded to nominal). If the degraded state fails all nodes simultaneously and is not recoverable within the task duration, the completion probability of a critical task of duration  d  with redundancy  r  is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1 - p_{01})^d \, P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, d, p_0), \tag{F.3}

where  P_{\mathrm{ind}}  is the independent formula with nominal failure probability  p_0 .

Proof. The task completes iff (i) the chain stays nominal for all  d  steps, with probability  (1 - p_{01})^d , and (ii) at least one of the  r  replicas survives the nominal-state independent failures, with probability  P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, d, p_0) . The two events are independent given the chain path, and the product follows. If the chain enters the degraded state at any point during the task, all replicas fail simultaneously and the task fails.  \square 

Proposition F.5 (Weibull holding time). If the degraded state has Weibull holding time  F_{\mathrm{deg}}(t) = 1 - e^{-(t/\lambda)^k} , and a task can survive a degraded interval only if the interval ends before the task's critical phase, then

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \sum_{k=0}^{d} (1 - p_{01})^k p_{01} \, P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, k, p_0) \, P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d-k), \tag{F.4}

with  P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d-k) = 1 - F_{\mathrm{deg}}(d-k)  for  r \ge 1  and  d-k > 0 , and  P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, 0) = 1 .

Proof. Condition on the time  k  at which the chain first enters the degraded state. The probability of staying nominal for  k  steps and then entering degraded is  (1 - p_{01})^k p_{01} . The task survives the nominal phase with probability  P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, k, p_0) , and survives the degraded phase of duration  d - k  if the degraded state ends before the task's critical phase, with probability  1 - F_{\mathrm{deg}}(d-k) . Summing over  k  gives (F.4).  \square 

F.3 The  p_{\mathrm{eff}}  Caveat

Proposition F.6. Let  p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} p_0 + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} p_1 . Substituting  p_{\mathrm{eff}}  into the independent formula is conservative for  r = 1  but underestimates failure for  r > 1  under common cause.

Proof. For  r = 1 , the independent formula  P_{\mathrm{ind}}(1, d, p) = (1-p)^d  is convex in  p , so  \mathbb{E}[(1-p)^d] \ge (1 - \mathbb{E}[p])^d  by Jensen, giving a conservative bound. For  r > 1 , the independent formula assigns independent failures to the replicas, but under a common-cause event all replicas fail simultaneously. The independent formula with  p_{\mathrm{eff}}  therefore underestimates the failure probability.  \square 

F.4 Redundancy Threshold

For prescribed  \alpha ,  r^*(\alpha, d, p, \delta)  is the smallest  r  with  P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) \ge 1 - \alpha . Computed by binary search over  r , with the recursion (F.1) or (F.4) evaluated numerically. Complexity is  O(\log r_{\max} \cdot d)  per evaluation.

