Appendix G — Architecture and Algorithms

G.1 Trust Boundary

The validator is the sole component inside the trust boundary. It is deterministic, total, and sound with respect to the model. The estimator, baseline, optimizer, and LLM are outside and can only propose actions. The trust boundary is a one-way gate: proposals flow in, accept/reject decisions flow out. No proposal generator has a path to modify the validator, the safe set, or the disturbance bounds.

G.2 Validator Predicates

The validator is a function  \text{Validate} : (\hat{x}, a) \mapsto \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}\}  implemented as a conjunction:

1. Schema check: the action conforms to the declared schema.
2. Reference check: every task ID and node ID exists.
3. Robust admissibility check:  a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) .
4. Resource check: assigned work does not exceed  C_i .
5. Power check: instantaneous load does not exceed worst-case available power.
6. Energy check:  [\mathcal{R}_B](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [m_{t+1}, B^{\max}]  in interval arithmetic.
7. Thermal check:  [\mathcal{R}_T](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq (-\infty, T^\circ(a)]  in interval arithmetic.
8. Criticality check: critical tasks assigned to nodes whose availability is guaranteed for the task duration.

The validator returns accept only if all checks pass. Complexity is  O(N \cdot |Q|) .

G.3 LLM Interface

Five-stage pipeline: snapshot construction, prompt assembly, inference, parse and schema-validate, semantic and constraint validation. The LLM proposes candidate heuristics or parameter settings for the constrained optimizer, not direct schedules. Fallback is the deterministic constrained optimizer, which itself passes through the validator. If the fallback is rejected, the system enters the safe state.

G.4 MILP Formulation

Variables:  z_{j,i,t} \in \{0,1\} ,  B_{i,t} \ge 0 ,  T_{i,t}  (linearized),  D_j \ge 0 . Objective:

\max \sum_{j,i,t} v_j z_{j,i,t} - \lambda_E \sum_{i,t} P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{comp}} \Delta t - \lambda_D \sum_j D_j. \tag{G.1}

Constraints: assignment uniqueness, resource capacity, storage dynamics with shunt, instantaneous power limits, thermal limits, lateness definitions. For  N \le 5  and  |Q| \le 20 , solved exactly by branch-and-bound.

G.5 Lagrangian Relaxation

Relaxing the energy and thermal coupling constraints with multipliers  \lambda, \mu \ge 0 :

\mathcal{L}(z, \lambda, \mu) = \sum_j v_j z_j - \lambda^T g_E(z) - \mu^T g_T(z). \tag{G.2}

Dual function  g(\lambda, \mu) = \max_{z \in \mathcal{Z}} \mathcal{L}(z, \lambda, \mu)  decomposes by node. Subgradient ascent:

\lambda^{(k+1)} = \bigl[\lambda^{(k)} + \alpha_k g_E(z^{(k)})\bigr]_+, \qquad \mu^{(k+1)} = \bigl[\mu^{(k)} + \alpha_k g_T(z^{(k)})\bigr]_+. \tag{G.3}

Weak duality:  g(\lambda, \mu)  is an upper bound on the primal optimum. The duality gap is zero for the restricted single-node preemptive model (Theorem 9.3) and reported empirically for the general case.

G.6 Complexity

MILP:  O(N |Q| T)  binary variables, exponential in the worst case. Lagrangian:  O(N \cdot \mathrm{poly}(|Q|, T))  per iteration. Lyapunov:  O(N |Q|)  per step. Validator:  O(N |Q|)  per call. LLM: bounded by the token budget.

